  {
   "id": "syn-nvda-2",
   "text": "The US Commerce Department on Tuesday expanded restrictions on the sale of advanced semiconductors to China, closing routes that had allowed modified versions of high-end accelerators to be shipped there.\n\nThe rules take effect in thirty days. Nvidia, the largest maker of the chips covered, said in a filing that it expects a meaningful hit to data centre revenue in the region, which had accounted for a double-digit share of the segment, and that it would design products compliant with the new thresholds.\n\nAnalysts said the broader demand for the company's products from US and European cloud providers would absorb much of the shortfall, but that the move raises regulatory pressure on the whole sector and adds uncertainty to earnings forecasts for the coming year.\n\nShares of Nvidia and its competitors fell in early trading.",
